# Line endings, and why this file exists at all.
#
# This machine has core.autocrlf=true and there was no .gitattributes. A review
# proved the consequence rather than reasoning about it: commit
# spike/plugins/fixtures/build.gradle.pristine, clone the repo back, and the
# fixture arrives with CRLF terminators. withReleaseSigning.js matches that
# template with exact multi-line string equality built from \n literals, so both
# of its anchors miss, and the error it raises says "The Expo template changed.
# Re-read the file and update the anchors" -- pointing the reader at Expo when
# the real cause is their own checkout. A misdiagnosis is worse than a failure.
#
# The APK's signature was never at risk (spike/android/ is gitignored, so git
# never writes that file and prebuild emits LF), but the gate died in any fresh
# clone, which includes the author's own next one.

* text=auto

# Byte-exact fixtures. These are compared with whole-string equality, so a
# line-ending rewrite is a content change. Never normalise them.
*.pristine -text

# Shell scripts must be LF even on Windows: the device harness in spike/tools/
# runs under Git Bash, and CRLF gives `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Gradle and properties files are read by the JVM on Windows, but the plugin
# does exact matching against build.gradle, so keep them LF for consistency
# with what Expo's prebuild writes.
*.gradle text eol=lf
